{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q015_artifact_tag_not_homology","answer":"In SCOPe 2.08, d1a14h2 (sunid 343393, "1a14 H:1-8") is not a biological protein domain. It is an engineered N-terminal tag. Its lineage is class l, Artifacts (sunid 310555) > fold l.1, Tags (310573) > superfamily l.1.1, Tags (310607) > family l.1.1.1, Tags (310682) > protein "N-terminal Tags" (310894) > species "Synthetic" (311501). From memory and not checked through the API: the 1a14 H chain is the VH half of the NC10 single-chain Fv bound to influenza neuraminidase, and an 8-residue N-terminal segment fits a FLAG-type (DYKDDDDK) expression/purification tag. SCOPe only says it is a synthetic N-terminal tag, and I did not check the sequence. The real immunoglobulin part of the chain is d1a14h1 (sunid 20061, H:9-111). It sits in class b (All beta proteins) > fold b.1 (Immunoglobulin-like beta-sandwich) > superfamily b.1.1 (Immunoglobulin) > family b.1.1.1 (V set domains) > protein VH (88543) > species Mouse (Mus musculus), cluster 3.2. Splitting the chain at residue 8/9 keeps the tag out of the immunoglobulin domain.

Similarity to d1a14h2 should not be treated as evidence of protein homology. The 2.08 homology endpoint returns homology_implied_by_level = false and the caution warning "artifact_not_homology_evidence": "Class l contains artificial sequence artifacts; similarity to these records does not imply biological homology." Class l, fold l.1, superfamily l.1.1 and family l.1.1.1 are bookkeeping groups for artifacts. They are not an evolutionary or structural class, fold, superfamily or family, so sharing the l.1.1.1 "family" with another tagged domain says nothing about common ancestry, structure or function. A BLAST or FATCAT hit that comes only from a shared tag (for example, another protein with the same FLAG or His tag) is an artifact of how the proteins were made. It does not transfer classification or function and must not support a homology or template claim.

The tag also says nothing for or against the homology of the real domain. Any relationship of 1a14 H to other immunoglobulins should rest on the b.1.1.1 domain d1a14h1 alone. When building benchmarks, homology searches or template sets, mask or exclude the tag residues and class-l domains, or keep them as a separate group with this caveat. The tag's annotation also includes the entry-level comment "complexed with ca, man, nag"; it is not a functional property of the tag.","evidence_used":["scop-homology/SKILL.md","scop-homology/references/biological-interpretation.md","scop-homology/references/api-usage.md","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1a14h2","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1a14h2/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1a14h2/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1a14h2/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1a14h1","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1a14h1/parents","pretrained knowledge: 1a14 is the NC10 scFv–neuraminidase complex; FLAG-tag identity is unverified"]}]}
